Consider the sign matrix $\mathbf{H}_j$ indexed by $\{0,1\}^j\times\{0,1\}^j$, with entries
\begin{equation}
\bigl[\mathbf{H}_j\bigr]_{\underline{b},\underline{s}}
=
(-1)^{\underline{b}\cdot\underline{s}},
\qquad
\underline{b},\underline{s}\in\{0,1\}^j .
\end{equation}
The rows of $\mathbf{H}_j$ are orthogonal, since
\begin{equation}
\sum_{\underline{s}\in\{0,1\}^j}
(-1)^{(\underline{b}\oplus\underline{b}')\cdot\underline{s}}
=
\begin{cases}
2^j, & \underline{b}=\underline{b}',\\
0, & \underline{b}\neq \underline{b}'.
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
Indeed, when $\underline{b}\neq \underline{b}'$, the nonzero linear form $(\underline{b}\oplus\underline{b}')\cdot\underline{s}$ takes the values $0$ and $1$ equally often as $\underline{s}$ ranges over $\{0,1\}^j$, so the corresponding signs cancel.
Thus $\mathbf{H}_j\mathbf{H}_j^{\mathsf T}=2^j\mathbf{I}$, and $\mathbf{H}_j$ is nonsingular.

We now relate the rows of $\mathbf{B}_j$ to the rows of $\mathbf{H}_j$. For the all-zero row index,
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{B}_j(\underline{0},\cdot)
